% ============================================================================
% landform-extent — for the landforms whose SAME cited USGS thesaurus span
% also states an "extent" (size) descriptor, that descriptor
% (adj-facts-stdlib, GEOGRAPHY).
% ============================================================================
% A sibling to the already-shipped `landforms.adj` (`landform_description
% (landform, description)`, ONE defining descriptor per landform: mountain,
% valley, plateau, plain, canyon). That table's own header already quotes,
% VERBATIM, the FULL USGS Feature Type Thesaurus span for every row — but
% the descriptor-only schema reduced each span to a single atom, leaving a
% distinct "extent" clause unused for two of the five landforms:
%
%   plateau → great_extent
%     "Comparatively flat areas of great extent and elevation..."
%
%   plain → considerable_extent
%     "Regions of general uniform slope, comparatively level and of
%      considerable extent."
%
% "Extent" (how much area the landform covers) is categorically distinct
% from the parent table's own descriptor for each: plateau's descriptor is
% flat_elevated (about being FLAT and ELEVATED, not about SIZE), and plain's
% descriptor is comparatively_level (about being LEVEL, not about SIZE) — so
% decoding the extent clause is a genuinely separate fact, not a restatement.
%
% Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? landform_extent(plateau, $Extent)   % great_extent
%
% This is a native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5, a TWO-column table like
% `landforms.adj`'s own `landform_description`): each `row` lowers to a
% ground relation `landform_extent(landform, extent)` carrying the citation,
% so the lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding query — the engine returns
% the extent WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero model calls, and
% abstains on mountain, valley, and canyon, whose own cited spans never use
% the word "extent" for their size (mountain and canyon do not discuss
% areal extent at all; valley's span says "relatively large", a distinct,
% vaguer word the source itself does not tie to "extent"). The query also
% runs BACKWARD — bind an extent and recall which landform it names:
%
%     ? landform_extent($Landform, considerable_extent)   % plain
%
% Provenance: reproduces, byte-for-byte, the SAME two spans already quoted
% inside `landforms.adj`'s own header, from the SAME USGS-hosted Feature
% Type Thesaurus page that table already cites — no new WebFetch, only a
% different schema decoding the extent-clause half of each already-verified
% quote. `trust authoritative` — the same tier `landforms.adj` already
% carries (USGS, a U.S. federal .gov source).
% (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table landform_extent {
    columns landform, extent

    row (plateau, great_extent)          % USGS Feature Type Thesaurus
    row (plain, considerable_extent)     % USGS Feature Type Thesaurus

    source "Comparatively flat areas of great extent and elevation; specif. extensive land regions considerably above the adjacent country or above sea level; commonly limited on at least one side by an abrupt descent ..."
    locator "https://apps.usgs.gov/thesaurus/thesaurus-full.php?thcode=3"
    trust authoritative

    cites "Regions of general uniform slope, comparatively level and of considerable extent." locator "https://apps.usgs.gov/thesaurus/thesaurus-full.php?thcode=3"
}
